\begin{abstract}
High-throughput industrial human-robot collaboration (HRC) demands sub-second dynamic trajectory adaptation governed by real-time safety metrics. However, traditional ISO/TS 15066 speed and separation monitoring (SSM) treats human operators as static rigid obstacles, leading to sudden kinematic jerks and worker mistrust. Deep neural networks predicting operator intent often function as uninterpretable black boxes, precluding certification in safety-critical assembly. This paper presents an Explainable AI (XAI) supervisory framework that couples bio-mechanical ergonomics with deterministic trajectory scaling. An 8-dimensional telemetry vector ingesting spatial proximity ($d_{\min}$), eye closure ratio ($\text{PERCLOS}$), postural strain, and handover torque ($\tau_h$) is evaluated using an additive Kernel SHAP architecture alongside a closed-form velocity governor. The resulting framework provides real-time semantic alert codes on operator handover panels in $<2.8$\,ms on standard edge CPUs, maintaining ISO compliance while eliminating opaque robot deceleration.
\end{abstract}

